<%= form_with model: @teacher, url: @teacher.persisted? ? admin_teacher_path(@teacher) : admin_teachers_path, builder: Ui::FormBuilder, class: "space-y-6" do |f| %>
  <%= render "shared/form_errors", record: @teacher, subject: "teacher" %>

  <%# `components/ui/_card`, and no heading of its own.

      The surface was hand-rolled in ten form partials - `<div class="tw-card"><div class="p-5">` - so it
      was declared eleven times, which is the mechanism that produced four different padding values before
      anyone noticed. And each opened with an `h3` naming the record type, under a page header that had
      just named it: "Teacher details" below "Edit teacher", 24px apart, in two sizes. Polaris drops a
      card's header when the page has one card. A heading returns the moment a form has two - see
      admin/students, where it separates them. %>
  <%= render layout: "components/ui/card", locals: {} do %>
    <%# **Two sentences on this card are true on one of the two pages that render it and false on the
        other**, so they branch on `persisted?` rather than being written for whichever page was open when
        they were drafted:

        - Saving a *new* teacher emails a setup link - `create` calls `send_reset_password_instructions`.
          Saving an existing one emails nothing, and `:confirmable` is not among the devise modules, so
          even changing the address is silent.
        - Only the create page's description says a teacher can hold classrooms in two schools. The record
          page's description is the teacher's own summary line, so there the group hint is the only place
          that fact can live - and on create it would be the second copy of it. %>
    <% creating = !@teacher.persisted? %>

    <% email_hint = if creating
                      "Used to sign in. A link to set a password will be sent by email once saved."
                    else
                      "Used to sign in. Changing it changes how this teacher signs in."
                    end %>

    <div>
        <%# Two facts, both about this field: it is the login username - `sync_username_from_email` copies
            it, so the sign-in form's "username" is this address - and, on create, where the setup mail
            goes. The second used to be the page's description, which read as a footnote about the future:
            the reader met "a reset email goes to this address" before there was an address.

            **The mail does not carry the temporary password, and this said it did.** `create` generates one
            with `Devise.friendly_token` so the record can save; what is sent is Devise's reset mail, which
            is a link and nothing else. Nobody ever sees that password, the teacher included. %>
        <%= f.text_field :email,
                         label: "Email",
                         required: true,
                         hint: email_hint,
                         placeholder: "teacher@example.com" %>

        <%# "Teacher's full name" restated the label and the placeholder already shows the shape. What a
            reader cannot see is that the field is optional and what happens if they leave it: `display_name`
            falls back to the email's local part, and it is what the index, the record page's title and every
            classroom teacher list render. %>
        <%= f.text_field :name,
                         label: "Full name",
                         width: :half,
                         hint: "Displayed wherever this teacher appears. If left blank, the first " \
                               "part of their email is shown instead.",
                         placeholder: "John Doe" %>

        <%# **The school filter is gone, and the list says which school each classroom is in.**
            It was a single-select dropdown above a multi-select group, both about classrooms, telling them
            apart by label alone - and "Show classrooms from" named a mechanism rather than a thing. What it
            did was reload the group into a turbo frame with a narrower list, defaulting to the school of the
            teacher's *first* classroom, which is how it once dropped the second school's classroom on save.

            It was hiding a list shorter than itself: the group is already limited to active classrooms in
            the current school year. `classroom_option_label` puts the school on each row, so two schools
            running a "Grade 5" are still distinguishable, and a teacher holding classrooms in both can see
            it rather than read it in a caveat.

            The threshold for changing the control is the one classrooms#form states: past roughly ten
            options a checkbox list becomes a searchable multi-select with chips. A dropdown that hides
            options is not that control. %>
        <div class="mb-6">
          <% if @classrooms.any? %>
            <%= f.collection_check_boxes :classroom_ids,
                                         @classrooms,
                                         :id,
                                         classroom_option_label,
                                         label: "Classrooms",
                                         hint: "Select all classrooms this teacher teaches.",
                                         wrapper_class: "mb-0" %>
          <% else %>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-slate-700 mb-2">Classrooms</label>
            <%# The callout, not a hand-rolled panel with an inline SVG and yellow-400/700/800 ink.
                The link is the callout's trailing action rather than a link buried mid-sentence:
                a notice plus the thing to do about it. yellow was also one step off the amber the
                component uses.

                It named the school filter - "associated with this school and active year" - and there is no
                school filter now. The year is the only thing narrowing the list. %>
            <%= render layout: "components/ui/callout",
                       locals: { title: "No classrooms available",
                                 body: "No classrooms have been set up for the current school year yet." } do %>
              <%= link_to admin_classrooms_path, class: ghost_class do %>
                <%= lucide_icon "arrow-right", class: "size-4 shrink-0" %>
                Update classrooms
              <% end %>
            <% end %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
    </div>

      <%# The "a temporary password is generated and a reset email is sent" line used to sit here, below the
          last field, where a reader met it after deciding what to type. It went to the page description,
          which was no better - a footnote about the future, above the field it was about - and it is the
          **email field's** hint now. What happens on submit belongs on the input that causes it. %>
  <% end %>

  <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-x-4">
    <%# Primary first, then cancel. design.md sets that order for a leading-edge row - it is
        GOV.UK's and Material's - and this was the one form in the product with them the other way round,
        because `form_actions_test` asserts the order on the teacher-facing forms and not on this one. %>
    <div class="tw-form-actions">
      <%= f.submit_button @teacher.persisted? ? "Update teacher" : "Create teacher" %>
      <%= f.cancel_button "Cancel", url: @teacher.persisted? ? admin_teacher_path(@teacher) : admin_teachers_path %>
    </div>
    <% if @teacher.discarded? %>
      <%= link_to "Permanently delete",
                  admin_teacher_path(@teacher),
                  data: { turbo_method: :delete, turbo_confirm: teacher_delete_confirm(@teacher) },
                  class: admin_danger_button_class %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
